{"actor":"server","event":"response","topic_id":"2024-23862","model_call":8,"request":{"op":"query","query":"(^ \"highway trust fund\" (+ diverted diversion raid raided misuse misused) (+ state states))"},"record":{"query":{"op":"query","query":"(^ \"highway trust fund\" (+ diverted diversion raid raided misuse misused) (+ state states))"},"response":{"burrow":"/data/ssd3/claclark/msm-2.1.burrow","docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_01_2112008170#6_3194393002","ok":true,"op":"query","qid":"q3222","rank":1,"snippet":"in 1940. The Pennsylvania Turnpike was financed through a public authority known as the Pennsylvania Turnpike Commission, while the Connecticut Highway Department sold bonds to private investors to finance construction of the Merritt Parkway. Freeway construction ceased during the early 1940s as all available resources were <cover>diverted to win World War II. After the war was over, freeway construction resumed, with public authorities overseeing tollways and toll bridges in Connecticut, Delaware, Florida, Illinois, Indiana, Kansas, Kentucky, Maine, Maryland, Massachusetts, New Hampshire, New Jersey, New York, Ohio, Oklahoma, Pennsylvania, and West Virginia. All of these turnpikes offered motorists efficient high-speed travel over long distances, while being financially self-sustaining through the collection of tolls. Era of Interstate Highways and the Federal Highway Trust Fund Planning for a national system of freeways had been ongoing since the 1930s, but the first legislation that designated specific routes for future Interstate highways was the Federal Aid Highway Act of 1944.\" , \"start_char\": 4594.000000 , \"title\": \"Highway Financing in the United States - </cover>America's Lost Roads\" , \"url\": \"http://lostroads.wikidot.com/future-highway-financing\" }"},"time":75},"model_calls":8,"document_judgments":6,"relevance_3":2,"max_document_judgments":50,"max_model_calls":100}
