{"actor":"server","event":"response","topic_id":"2024-23862","model_call":19,"request":{"op":"query","query":"(^ california \"high-speed rail\" (+ audit auditor) (+ mismanagement mismanaged waste wasted cost overruns) (+ funds funding dollars))"},"record":{"query":{"op":"query","query":"(^ california \"high-speed rail\" (+ audit auditor) (+ mismanagement mismanaged waste wasted cost overruns) (+ funds funding dollars))"},"response":{"burrow":"/data/ssd3/claclark/msm-2.1.burrow","docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_42_1597252022#4_3799450565","ok":true,"op":"query","qid":"q3231","rank":1,"snippet":"Nowhere After Spending $5.4 Billion, California’s Bullet Train Is Still Going Nowhere\" , \"segment\": \"Gross project mismanagement is to blame for some of the comical cost <cover>overruns that the state now confronts. California’s state auditor released a report last month that doesn’t sugarcoat the culpability of the state’s planning agencies. These mistakes range from awarding construction contracts before securing the land needed for the project, to failing to determine how utilities would be relocated, to making key decisions before all parties involved had signed agreements. Not surprisingly, litigation runs amok over this mess. It didn’t have to be this way. California could have learned much from Texas, which is also developing high-speed rail. The Texas project will connect Houston and Dallas, along with one intermediate stop (College Station, Texas, which is the home of Texas A&M University) between the two cities. A major difference between the two projects is that private investment is funding </cover>the Texas project, which has about a $15 billion price tag. Less litigation, fewer regulations, and picking a route that is relatively easy to build are all important reasons why the Texas project "},"time":188},"model_calls":19,"document_judgments":12,"relevance_3":4,"max_document_judgments":50,"max_model_calls":100}
