{"actor":"server","event":"response","topic_id":"2024-23862","model_call":43,"request":{"op":"next","qid":"q3254"},"record":{"query":{"op":"next","qid":"q3254"},"response":{"burrow":"/data/ssd3/claclark/msm-2.1.burrow","docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_27_348866207#8_677714515","ok":true,"op":"next","qid":"q3254","rank":5,"snippet":"{ \"docid\": \"msmarco_v2.1_doc_27_348866207#8_677714515\" , \"end_char\": 7219.000000 , \"headings\": \"How Much Gas Tax Money States Divert Away From Roads Policy Brief How Much <cover>Gas Tax Money States Divert Away From Roads Examining the percentage of state gas tax revenue that is allocated for expenses unrelated to roads, including money shifted to law enforcement, education, tourism, environmental programs and more. State Gas Tax Diversion </cover>Rates Background Figure 1: State Gas Tax Rates 2000-2019 Gas Tax Diversions by State Figure 2: State Gas Tax Diversion Rates Figure 3: Average Diversion Rate of Each U.S. Region Methodology Colorado Connecticut Florida Kansas Kentucky Louisiana Maine Maryland Massachusetts Michigan New Jersey (FY18) New York North Carolina North Dakota Oklahoma Pennsylvania Rhode Island South Carolina South Dakota Texas Utah Vermont Virginia Washington Wisconsin Analysis Recommendations \" , \"segment\": \"Rather than dedicating all gas tax revenue to roads and highways, many states divert portions of the revenue to non-road and non-transportation purposes. While those paying gas taxes may see some benefit in non-road and non-transportation ","skipped":[{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_27_348866207#8_677714515","rank":5,"relevance":3}]},"time":4},"model_calls":43,"document_judgments":24,"relevance_3":8,"max_document_judgments":50,"max_model_calls":100}
{"actor":"server","event":"request","topic_id":"2024-23862","model_call":43,"request":{"op":"next","qid":"q3254"},"model_calls":43,"document_judgments":24,"relevance_3":8,"max_document_judgments":50,"max_model_calls":100}
{"actor":"server","event":"response","topic_id":"2024-23862","model_call":43,"request":{"op":"next","qid":"q3254"},"record":{"query":{"op":"next","qid":"q3254"},"response":{"burrow":"/data/ssd3/claclark/msm-2.1.burrow","docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_27_348866207#1_677697056","ok":true,"op":"next","qid":"q3254","rank":6,"snippet":"MFT) revenue to transit. Ten states divert a portion of their gas tax revenue to law enforcement and safety services, marking the second most common diversion. Though less frequent, diversions to education tend to be substantial, accounting for 25.9% and 24.7% of gas tax revenue in Michigan and Texas respectively. Other states divert gas tax revenue to tourism, environmental programs and administrative costs. In total, 22 states divert over 1% of their gas tax revenue. By violating the users-pay/users-benefit principle, <cover>diversion poses both immediate and long-term threats to transportation funding. Diversions can leave roads and highways underfunded. The 10 states diverting the largest percentage of their gas tax money: </cover>New York diverts 37.5% of its gas tax revenue, Rhode Island diverts 37.1%, New Jersey and Michigan divert 33.9%, Maryland diverts 32.5%, Connecticut diverts 27%, Texas diverts 24%, Massachusetts diverts 23.9%, Florida diverts 13.6% and Vermont diverts 13.2%. This policy brief catalogs state gas tax diversions of the 25 states that employ that practice and outlines potential policies that will strengthen the users-pay/users-benefit model of transportation funding.\" , \"start_char\": ","skipped":[{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_27_348866207#0_677694195","rank":1,"relevance":3},{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_27_348866207#2_677699508","rank":2,"relevance":3},{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_27_348866207#10_677719588","rank":3,"relevance":3},{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_27_348866207#9_677717302","rank":4,"relevance":3},{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_27_348866207#8_677714515","rank":5,"relevance":3}]},"time":4},"model_calls":43,"document_judgments":24,"relevance_3":8,"max_document_judgments":50,"max_model_calls":100}
