{"actor":"server","event":"response","topic_id":"2024-23862","model_call":67,"request":{"op":"next","qid":"q3269"},"record":{"query":{"op":"next","qid":"q3269"},"response":{"burrow":"/data/ssd3/claclark/msm-2.1.burrow","docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_28_374637909#12_785213496","ok":true,"op":"next","qid":"q3269","rank":5,"snippet":"Every City Companies Need to Work With Cities Riding Into the Future \" , \"segment\": \"However, a redefined future of <cover>transportation means cities’ revenues will change, and in the short run they’ll likely decline. LA, for example, brings in over $100 million of revenue annually from DMV fees, parking fees, gas tax, and traffic violations. Cities won’t happily kiss all that revenue goodbye, and citizens need it, too, since some of it goes to schools, infrastructure, and services like police. Infrastructure itself will also have to change to accommodate, for example, more pickups and dropoffs, and a decreased need for parking. Lyft hopes to work on all these issues in concert with the cities where it operates. “ You can’t be fighting the city,” Kapoor said. “ You need to work with the city, and make it work for everyone that’s there.” Riding Into the Future Besides disrupting city revenues and infrastructure, the advent of electric self-driving rideshares will have massive second-order effects. “ Insurance, auto loans, used </cover>cars, rentals—all those things will be impacted to the tune of two trillion dollars in the US alone,” Kapoor said. Lyft, Uber, and other "},"time":15},"model_calls":67,"document_judgments":42,"relevance_3":16,"max_document_judgments":50,"max_model_calls":100}
