{"actor":"server","event":"response","topic_id":"2024-23862","model_call":71,"request":{"op":"query","query":"(^ \"highway user\" (+ revenues revenue fees taxes) (+ diverted diversion) (+ state states) (+ roads highways))"},"record":{"query":{"op":"query","query":"(^ \"highway user\" (+ revenues revenue fees taxes) (+ diverted diversion) (+ state states) (+ roads highways))"},"response":{"burrow":"/data/ssd3/claclark/msm-2.1.burrow","docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_03_350022295#4_649551864","ok":true,"op":"query","qid":"q3271","rank":1,"snippet":"Transport Costs Per Passenger Mile About The Antiplanner \" , \"segment\": \"So transit costs 84 cents per passenger mile, nearly four times the cost of driving. Transit fares average 20.4 cents per passenger mile (interesting how the transit industry keeps fares close to the actual cost of driving), so the subsidies are nearly 64 cents per mile. How much are the subsidies to driving? That answer is also available from the Federal Highway Administration. In 2005, federal, state, and (mostly) local governments spent $39.2 billion in non-<cover>highway user fees on roads. Offsetting this, federal, local, and (mostly) state governments diverted $</cover>21.3 billion from highway user fees into non-highway programs. So the net subsidy was $17.9 billion. ( By comparison, transit received $30 billion in subsidies in 2005.) The highway subsidy works out to a subsidy of 0.38 cents per passenger mile. Added to the consumer cost and the total cost of driving is 22.4 cents per passenger mile — still a lot less than transit costs.\" , \"start_char\": 2863.000000 , \"title\": \"Transport Costs Per Passenger Mile – The Antiplanner"},"time":135},"model_calls":71,"document_judgments":45,"relevance_3":18,"max_document_judgments":50,"max_model_calls":100}
